# {{lb|en|often|mining|waste management}} The [[breaking#Noun|breaking]] or [[grinding#Noun|grinding]] up of a material to form smaller [[particle]]s.
#* {{quote-journal|title=VI. An Account of a Book. ''Novæ'' Hypotheseos, ''ad explicanda febrium intermittentium Symptomata & Typos excogitatæ'' Hypotyposis. ''Unà cum Ætiologià Remediorium; speciatim vero de curatione per'' Corticem Peruvianum. ''Accessit Dissertatiuncula de Intestinorum motu Peristaltico. Authore'' Guilielmo Cole, M.D. Lond. in 8º. 1693.|journal=[[w:Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society|Philosophical Transactions]]|location=London|publisher=Printed for, and sold by Samuel Smith, printer to the {{w|Royal Society}}, at the Princes Arms in [[w:St Paul's Cathedral|St. Paul's Church-yard]]|month=February|year=1692–1693|year_published=1693|issue=197|page=652|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=29NeAAAAcAAJ&pg=RA1-PA661-IA1|oclc=630046584|passage=He deſcribes in a Mechanical way the ''manner of its'' Action; ſuppoſing that ſome of its Particles having undergone ſeveral '''Comminutions''' (tho not a Diſſolution) from the various Firments they have paſſed, arrive at laſt, with the Blood, at the Brain; {{...}} thoſe of them, which are not ſmall enough to paſs thoſe Straits along with the Juice, being juſt admitted, ſtick there till other appelling Subſtances give them a farther '''Comminution''', and ſo pretrude them along: {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|author=William Cowper|authorlink=William Cowper (anatomist)|title=IV. An Account of Chylification|journal=[[w:Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society|Philosophical Transactions. Giving Some Accompt of the Present Undertakings, Studies, and Labours, of the Ingenious, in Many Considerable Parts of the World]]|location=Oxford|publisher=Printed at the Theater, and are to be sold by Samuel Smith, at the Prince's Arms in [[w:St Paul's Cathedral|S{{sup|t}} Paul's Church-yard]], London; and Henry Clements, bookseller in Oxford|month=March–May|year=1696|volume=XIX|issue=220|pages=231–232|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/philosophicaltra1916roya#page/232/mode/1up|oclc=630046584|passage=[W]hen a proportionable piece [of an aliment (food item)] is thus taken into the Mouth, the lower Jaw is variouſly mov'd by its proper Muſcles, and Maſtication is begun and carryed on by the Aſſiſtance of the Tongue, Cheeks, and Lips; the two firſt ſtill applying the leſs divided parts of the Aliment, to the ''Dentes Molares'' [molar teeth], till there's an equal '''Comminution''' of all its Parts; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|author=W[illiam] Derham|authorlink=William Derham|chapter=Of the Food of Animals|title=Physico-theology: Or, A Demonstration of the Being and Attributes of God, from His Works of Creation. Being the Substance of Sixteen Sermons Preached in St. Mary le Bow-Church, London, at the Honourable Mr. Boyle’s Lectures, in the Year 1711 and 1712. With Large Notes, and Many Curious Observations|edition=3rd corrected|location=London|publisher=Printed for W. Innys, at the Prince's-Arms in [[w:St Paul's Cathedral|St. Paul's Church Yard]]|year=1714|page=193|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=cM9XAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA193|oclc=642395327|passage=It's [the mouth's] hooked Form is of great Uſe to the rapacious Kind [of bird], in catching and holding their Prey, and in the '''Comminution''' thereof by tearing; to others it is no less ſerviceable to their climbing, as well as neat and nice '''Comminution''' of their Food.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Andrew Brown|chapter=13|title=The Philosophy of Physics: Or, Process of Creative Development, by which the First Principles of Physics are Proved beyond Controversy, and Their Effect in the Formation of All Physical Things Made Comprehensible to All Intelligent Minds, as in Phenomenal Nature|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[J. S.] Redfield, 110 & 112 [[w:Nassau Street (Manhattan)|Nassau Street]]|year=1854|page=417|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=AhBKAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA417|oclc=3694778|passage=[B]y what process, we would ask, could these sands have been sifted and separated agreeable to their grades, as they are now found? What circumstances could have effected such '''comminutions''', such separations, such transpositions, and transportations of the several kinds, having all the same origin?}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Barnard S. Proctor|chapter=Lecture II. Comminution.|title=Lectures on Practical Pharmacy|location=London|publisher=[[w:John Churchill (publisher)|J[ohn] & A[ugustus] Churchill]], {{w|New Burlington Street}}|year=1873|page=28|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=tjQD7W5SNawC&pg=PA28|oclc=4696801|passage=What circumstances determine the degree of '''comminution''' to which a substance should be subjected preparatory to future extraction?}}
#* {{quote-book|author=J. P. La Fage|author2=W. L. Nutting|chapter=Nutrient Dynamics of Termites|editor=M. V. Brian|title=Production Ecology of Ants and Termites|series=International Biological Programme|seriesvolume=13|location=Cambridge; New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Cambridge University Press}}|year=1978|page=165|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=4EA9AAAAIAAJ&pg=PA165|isbn=978-0-521-21519-0|passage=Termites are herbivores and detritivores variously involved in the '''comminution''' and decomposition of vegetable matter, through most of the warm temperate and tropical zones.}}
# {{lb|en|traumatology}} The [[fracture#Noun|fracture]] of a [[bone#Noun|bone]] [[site#Noun|site]] in [[multiple]] [[piece#Noun|pieces]] (technically, at least three); [[crumbling#Noun|crumbling]].
#* {{quote-book|author=Friedrich Esmarch|chapter=On the Treatment of These Injuries|title=On Resection in Gun-shot Injuries. Observation and Experience in the Schleswig-Holstein Campaigns of 1848 to 1851|location=Kiel, Schleswig-Holstein, Germany|publisher=Carl Schroder & Co.|year=1851|section=part I (On the Injuries of Shafts of Bones by Bullets)|page=56|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=uv5ZAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA56|oclc=78249921|passage='''Comminution''' of the shaft of one or of both bones of the fore-arm does not of itself indicate operative interference, other complications may necessitate primary or secondary amputation.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Ping-Chung Leung|author2=Kwong-Sui Leung|author3=Leung-Kim Hung|chapter=Hand and Wrist Injuries|editor=Nikolaj Wolfson|title=Orthpedics in Disasters: Orthopedic Injuries in Natural Disasters and Mass Casualty Events|location=Heidelberg|publisher={{w|Springer Nature}}|year=2016|section=part IV (Treatment of Orthopaedic Injuries: Specific Orthopaedic Injuries), section 27.6.1 (Distal Radius Fracture)|page=300|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=8Y5FDAAAQBAJ&pg=PA300|column=2|doi=10.1007/978-3-662=48950-5|isbn=978-3-662-48948-2|passage=Wrist fractures encountered in natural disasters most frequently belong to the more complicated types, viz. III, IV and VIII. Importantly, '''comminutions''' are common. When both radius and ulnar{{sic|ulna}} are involved with '''comminutions''', joint surface involvements are often combined with soft tissue injuries.}}

====Usage notes====
In [[traumatology]], comminution is to be distinguished from a [[compound#Adjective|compound]] or [[open]] [[fracture#Noun|fracture]], though fractures which are both comminuted and compound do occur.

